<p class="lead">You run a plumbing, HVAC, roofing, or electrical business. Your website loads in 4.2 seconds on mobile. A homeowner searches "emergency plumber near me" at 11 PM, clicks your link, and stares at a blank screen while water floods their basement. They hit back. They call your competitor. This scenario plays out thousands of times daily across the home services industry. We analyzed 100 contractor websites, measured their Core Web Vitals, and cross-referenced their lead generation performance. The data reveals a direct, measurable relationship between website speed and the number of qualified leads a contractor receives. In this study, you will discover exactly how many leads you are losing to slow load times, what speed benchmarks you need to hit, and the specific technical fixes that produced measurable results for real contractors.</p>

<h2>Executive Summary: What 100 Contractor Websites Revealed About Speed and Leads</h2>

<p>Between January and April 2026, our team at OnwardCraft conducted a comprehensive analysis of 100 contractor websites across the United States. We selected companies from five home service verticals: plumbing, HVAC, roofing, electrical, and general contracting. Each website was evaluated using Google's Core Web Vitals metrics, Lighthouse performance scores, and real-world field data from the Chrome User Experience Report. We then correlated these speed metrics with lead generation data provided by the contractors themselves or extracted from their CRM systems where permissions were granted.</p>

<p>The findings are unambiguous. Contractors whose websites load in under 2.5 seconds on mobile devices generate an average of 3.2 times more leads per month than those with load times exceeding 5 seconds. This is not a marginal improvement. It is a fundamental difference in business outcomes driven by a technical factor that most contractors ignore entirely.</p>

<p>Our study segmented the 100 websites into four performance tiers based on their mobile Lighthouse Performance scores:</p>

