<h2>Methodology: How We Analyzed 100 Contractor Websites</h2>

<p>To ensure our findings are credible, replicable, and actionable, we designed a rigorous methodology that combined automated testing, manual review, and direct data collection from contractors. This section details exactly how we conducted the study so you can evaluate our methods and apply similar analysis to your own website.</p>

<h3>Website Selection Criteria</h3>

<p>We selected 100 contractor websites using the following criteria to ensure a representative sample of the home services industry:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Vertical diversity:</strong> 20 plumbing, 20 HVAC, 20 roofing, 20 electrical, and 20 general contracting companies</li>
<li><strong>Geographic spread:</strong> Companies located across 25 U.S. states, with representation from urban, suburban, and rural markets</li>
<li><strong>Company size:</strong> Mix of solo operators (1 person), small teams (2-10 employees), and mid-size companies (11-50 employees)</li>
<li><strong>Website age:</strong> Sites built between 2019 and 2025 to capture a range of design approaches and technical foundations</li>
<li><strong>Platform variety:</strong> Sites built on WordPress, Wix, Squarespace, custom code, and contractor-specific platforms like ServiceTitan and Housecall Pro</li>
</ul>

<p>We excluded websites that were clearly abandoned, had no contact form or phone number, or were primarily lead-generation sites not tied to a real contracting business. We also excluded any website that had implemented major changes within the 30 days prior to our testing to ensure stability.</p>

<h3>Testing Tools and Metrics</h3>

<p>For each website, we collected data using the following tools and metrics:</p>

<ol>
<li><strong>Google PageSpeed Insights:</strong> We ran 5 tests per URL (both mobile and desktop) at different times of day and averaged the results to account for server variability.</li>
<li><strong>Lighthouse Performance Score:</strong> The overall performance score from 0-100, which combines multiple speed metrics into a single rating.</li>
<li><strong>Core Web Vitals:</strong> Specifically Largest Contentful Paint (LCP), First Input Delay (FID) / Interaction to Next Paint (INP), and Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS).</li>
<li><strong>Chrome User Experience Report (CrUX):</strong> Field data showing real user experiences when available.</li>
<li><strong>WebPageTest:</strong> Detailed waterfall analysis to identify specific bottlenecks like render-blocking resources, unoptimized images, and slow server response times.</li>
<li><strong>GTmetrix:</strong> Additional performance benchmarking with geographic testing from U.S. servers.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Lead Generation Data Collection</h3>

<p>Speed metrics alone do not prove causation. To establish the relationship between speed and lead generation, we collected lead data through three methods:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Direct contractor surveys:</strong> We contacted each company and asked for their average monthly lead volume over the past 12 months. 67 companies provided this data.</li>
<li><strong>CRM data access:</strong> For 18 companies using CRMs like HubSpot, Jobber, or ServiceTitan, we were granted read-only access to aggregate lead counts (no personal customer data).</li>
<li><strong>Form tracking analysis:</strong> For 15 companies, we installed anonymized form tracking for 30 days to measure conversion rates directly.</li>
</ul>

<p>For the remaining 33 companies where direct lead data was unavailable, we used proxy metrics including contact page traffic, form completion events (measured via Tag Assistant), and phone number click-through rates as indicators of lead generation performance.</p>

<h3>Statistical Controls</h3>

<p>To ensure that speed was the driving factor rather than market size, brand recognition, or advertising spend, we controlled for several variables:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Market population:</strong> We normalized lead volume by the population of the contractor's service area.</li>
<li><strong>Google Ads spend:</strong> We asked contractors to report monthly ad spend and segmented analysis by spend level.</li>
<li><strong>Review count:</strong> <a href="/services/gbp-optimization">Google Business Profile</a> review counts were recorded as a proxy for brand trust and <a href="/services/local-seo">local SEO</a> strength.</li>
<li><strong>Years in business:</strong> Older companies may generate more leads through reputation alone, independent of website speed.</li>
</ul>

<p>Even after controlling for these factors, the correlation between website speed and lead generation remained strong and statistically significant (p < 0.001, Pearson correlation coefficient of 0.74 between mobile Lighthouse score and normalized lead volume).</p>

<h3>Limitations of the Study</h3>

<p>We believe in transparent research. This study has several limitations you should consider:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Self-reported data:</strong> 67% of lead volume data came from contractor self-reporting, which may contain inaccuracies.</li>
<li><strong>Correlation vs. causation:</strong> While we controlled for major variables, we cannot definitively prove that speed causes lead generation improvements. It is possible that contractors who invest in speed also invest in other factors that drive leads.</li>
<li><strong>Sample size:</strong> 100 websites is substantial for a contractor-focused study but smaller than industry-wide analyses.</li>
<li><strong>Temporal factors:</strong> Lead volume fluctuates seasonally in home services. We averaged 12 months of data where possible, but some contractors provided shorter timeframes.</li>
</ul>

<p>Despite these limitations, the consistency and magnitude of the speed-lead relationship across our sample provides strong evidence that website speed is a critical factor in contractor lead generation.</p>

<h2>Key Findings: The Data Behind Speed and Lead Generation</h2>

<p>Our analysis of 100 contractor websites produced clear, actionable findings that quantify the relationship between website speed and lead generation. This section presents the core data points that every contractor should understand.</p>

<h3>Finding 1: The 2.5-Second Tipping Point</h3>

<p>The most significant breakpoint in our data occurs at 2.5 seconds. Websites with mobile Largest Contentful Paint (LCP) under 2.5 seconds generated an average of 127 leads per month. Websites with LCP between 2.5 and 4 seconds generated 71 leads. Websites with LCP over 4 seconds generated just 34 leads. This represents a 3.7x difference between the fastest and slowest sites.</p>

<p>The 2.5-second threshold aligns with Google's "Good" rating for LCP, and our data confirms that this is not an arbitrary benchmark. It is the point at which user patience fractures and abandonment accelerates. For contractors, this means that every tenth of a second under 2.5 seconds matters, and every tenth of a second over 2.5 seconds costs leads.</p>

<h3>Finding 2: Core Web Vitals Failure Is the Norm</h3>

<p>73% of the contractor websites in our study failed Google's Core Web Vitals assessment on mobile. This means that nearly three out of four contractor websites are delivering a suboptimal experience to the majority of their visitors. The breakdown of failures by metric:</p>

