<p>The high failure rate on LCP and INP is particularly concerning because these metrics most directly impact the user experience. A slow LCP means the main content is invisible for too long. A poor INP means the site feels unresponsive when users try to interact with it.</p>

<h3>Finding 3: Image Optimization Is the Biggest Quick Win</h3>

<p>Across all 100 websites, unoptimized images were the single largest contributor to slow load times. 82% of sites served images in legacy formats (JPEG/PNG) when WebP or AVIF would reduce file sizes by 40-70%. 67% of sites served images at resolutions far larger than needed for their display size. 54% of sites did not implement lazy loading, meaning every image on the page loaded immediately, even images below the fold that the user might never see.</p>

<p>We calculated that contractors who fully optimized their images (format conversion, proper sizing, compression, and lazy loading) could reduce their LCP by an average of 1.2 seconds without changing any other aspect of their website.</p>

<h3>Finding 4: Third-Party Scripts Devastate Performance</h3>

<p>The average contractor website loaded 14 third-party scripts. These included analytics trackers, live chat widgets, review embeds, social media pixels, heatmap tools, and scheduling widgets. Each script adds HTTP requests, blocks rendering, and consumes processing power on mobile devices.</p>

<p>We found a direct correlation between third-party script count and poor performance. Sites with more than 12 third-party scripts had an average mobile Lighthouse score of 41. Sites with fewer than 6 scripts had an average score of 78. The worst offenders were chat widgets (adding 0.8-1.5 seconds to load time), review embeds from third-party platforms (adding 0.5-1.2 seconds), and multiple analytics trackers (adding 0.3-0.7 seconds each).</p>

<h3>Finding 5: Hosting Quality Explains 31% of Speed Variance</h3>

<p>We categorized the hosting providers used by the 100 websites into three tiers: budget shared hosting, managed WordPress hosting, and premium/VPS hosting. The results were stark:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Budget shared hosting:</strong> Average Time to First Byte (TTFB) of 1.4 seconds, average Lighthouse score of 48</li>
<li><strong>Managed WordPress hosting:</strong> Average TTFB of 0.6 seconds, average Lighthouse score of 71</li>
<li><strong>Premium/VPS hosting:</strong> Average TTFB of 0.3 seconds, average Lighthouse score of 84</li>
</ul>

<p>Time to First Byte measures how quickly the server responds to a request. A slow TTFB means the user is waiting before any content even begins to load. Contractors on budget hosting were losing up to 1.1 seconds before their website content started rendering. This is a server infrastructure problem, not a <a href="/services/website-design">website design</a> problem, and it is solvable by changing hosting providers.</p>

<h3>Finding 6: Platform Choice Matters Less Than Implementation</h3>

<p>We expected to find that certain website platforms dramatically outperformed others. The reality was more nuanced. WordPress sites ranged from Lighthouse scores of 23 to 96. Wix sites ranged from 31 to 78. Squarespace sites ranged from 45 to 82. Custom-coded sites ranged from 34 to 98.</p>

<p>The platform itself was less predictive of performance than how the platform was implemented. A well-optimized WordPress site on quality hosting with a lightweight theme outperformed a poorly implemented custom site every time. This means contractors should not assume they need to rebuild their website on a different platform to achieve speed. In most cases, optimization of the existing platform produces better results than migration.</p>

<h3>Finding 7: Local Market Competition Amplifies Speed's Impact</h3>

<p>In highly competitive markets (defined as markets with 25+ competing contractors within a 15-mile radius), the speed-lead correlation was even stronger. In these markets, the fastest website generated 4.1x more leads than the slowest. In less competitive markets (fewer than 10 competitors), the gap was 2.3x.</p>

<p>This suggests that speed acts as a tiebreaker. When homeowners have many options, they will not wait for a slow site. In markets with limited competition, homeowners may tolerate slower sites because they have fewer alternatives. For contractors in competitive urban and suburban markets, speed optimization is not optional. It is essential for survival.</p>

