<p>The data reveals two critical patterns. First, faster sites not only retain more visitors but also convert a higher percentage of those visitors into leads. Second, abandonment accelerates dramatically after 3 seconds. At 4-5 seconds, two-thirds of users who start a form will abandon it before completion.</p>

<h3>Mobile-Specific Speed Killers</h3>

<p>Our waterfall analysis identified the specific technical factors that disproportionately impact mobile performance:</p>

<p><strong>Oversized touch targets and bloated CSS:</strong> Many contractor websites load CSS frameworks designed for desktop layouts, then override them for mobile. This means mobile devices download and process styles they never use. The average contractor website loaded 187KB of CSS but only used 34KB for the mobile viewport.</p>

<p><strong>Unoptimized images for mobile screens:</strong> 71% of sites served the same image files to mobile and desktop users. A 2000-pixel-wide hero image designed for a desktop monitor was being downloaded and scaled down on a 375-pixel-wide phone screen. Responsive images using the srcset attribute would serve appropriately sized files for each device.</p>

<p><strong>Excessive JavaScript on mobile processors:</strong> Mobile CPUs are significantly less powerful than desktop CPUs. JavaScript that executes in 200ms on a desktop might take 800ms on a mid-range smartphone. Contractor websites using page builders, animation libraries, and tracking scripts create a crushing load on mobile processors.</p>

<p><strong>Pop-ups and interstitials:</strong> Many contractor sites use email capture pop-ups, cookie consent banners, and promotional overlays. On mobile, these elements often cover the entire screen, are difficult to dismiss, and add significant load time. Google's own research shows that intrusive interstitials increase bounce rates by 25-40% on mobile.</p>

<h3>The 3G and Slow 4G Reality</h3>

<p>While 5G gets the headlines, many homeowners still search on slower connections. We tested our 100 websites on simulated 3G and slow 4G networks using WebPageTest. On a simulated 3G connection, the average contractor website took 14.7 seconds to become interactive. On slow 4G, it took 8.3 seconds. These are not edge cases. Rural areas, older buildings, and congested networks all produce these conditions regularly.</p>

<p>Contractors who optimize for slow connections capture leads that their competitors miss. A homeowner in a basement with poor cell reception can still load a lightweight, optimized website. They cannot load a bloated, unoptimized one. Speed optimization is accessibility optimization. It ensures your website works for every potential customer, regardless of their connection quality.</p>

<h3>Mobile Page Speed and Local Pack Rankings</h3>

<p>Google's local pack results, the map and three business listings that appear for local searches, are heavily influenced by mobile performance. Google has stated that mobile speed is a factor in local ranking, and our data supports this. Contractors with mobile Lighthouse scores above 75 appeared in the local pack 2.3 times more frequently than those with scores below 50.</p>

<p>The local pack is the most valuable real estate in contractor SEO. For searches like "emergency plumber near me" or "HVAC repair [city]," the local pack appears above all organic results. Being in the top three generates the majority of clicks and calls. Mobile speed optimization is one of the most direct ways to improve local pack visibility.</p>

